    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ibadan came into being in 1829, in the turmoil that destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-ile">Old Oyo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijaye">Ijaye</a> and Owu, as a war camp of warriors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, Ifẹ and Ijebu on a forested range of hills at the fringe of the grassland.</li><li>Where most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> cities had hereditary kings, Ibadan was a military republic run by four chiefs, three offices open to all freeborn men and one reserved for women, and the warrior class was its dominant and wealthiest group.</li><li>In 1840 the armies of Ibadan decisively defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>'s southward advance, and between the 1860s and 1890s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> ruled an empire over much of northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ibadan's name is coined from 'Eba-Odan', 'by the edge of the meadow': the camp stood where the forest met the grassland, which made it both a strongpoint and a market for the goods of both zones.</p></aside>
    </section>

